CAT = "ukaplhPersonal"; SEG = "PER"
Q = [
("To which licensing authority do you apply for a personal licence?",
 "The one where you live", ["The one where you work", "Any authority you choose", "The one where you trained"],
 "You apply to the licensing authority for the area where you are ordinarily resident. Only someone who lives outside England and Wales can apply to any authority."),
("What is the minimum age to hold a personal licence?",
 "18", ["16", "21", "25"],
 "An applicant must be at least 18. The same minimum age applies to an individual who applies for a premises licence or gives a temporary event notice."),
("How long does a personal licence last once it is granted?",
 "Indefinitely", ["For 3 years", "For 5 years", "For 10 years"],
 "Personal licences used to need renewing every 10 years, but the Deregulation Act 2015 removed renewal. A licence now lasts until it is surrendered, revoked or forfeited."),
("Which document must an applicant send with a personal licence application?",
 "A basic criminal record check", ["A credit reference report", "A food hygiene certificate", "A letter from an employer"],
 "The application needs a basic criminal record disclosure, the accredited qualification certificate, photographs, proof of the right to work in the UK and the fee. A credit history is not relevant."),
("How long do the police have to object to a personal licence application after being notified?",
 "14 days", ["7 days", "21 days", "28 days"],
 "The licensing authority notifies the police when an applicant has a relevant or foreign offence, and the police have 14 days to object. If they object, a hearing is held."),
("On what grounds can the police object to a personal licence application?",
 "An unspent relevant offence", ["A record of unpaid debts", "A past fine for parking", "Little trade experience"],
 "The police may object if granting the licence would undermine the crime prevention objective because of a relevant or foreign offence. Experience and finances are not grounds for objection."),
("Besides a qualification, what must a personal licence applicant show?",
 "The right to work in the UK", ["A UK bank account in their name", "A job offer from licensed premises", "Five years' residence in the area"],
 "Since the Immigration Act 2016, an applicant must be entitled to work in the UK, and the Home Office can object on immigration grounds. A job offer is not required."),
("Which qualification must a personal licence applicant hold?",
 "An accredited licensing qualification", ["A Level 3 hospitality diploma", "A Level 2 food hygiene certificate", "A cellar management certificate"],
 "The applicant must hold a licensing qualification accredited by the Secretary of State, such as a Level 2 award for personal licence holders. Other hospitality certificates do not count."),
("A personal licence holder moves house. What must they do?",
 "Tell the issuing authority", ["Tell only the local police", "Apply for a new licence", "Tell only their employer"],
 "A change of name or address must be notified to the licensing authority that issued the licence as soon as reasonably practicable, with the licence so it can be updated. Failing to do so is an offence."),
("A personal licence holder moves to a different council area. Which authority now holds their record?",
 "The one that issued it", ["The one they move to", "The one where they work", "The Home Office"],
 "A personal licence stays with the authority that granted it, wherever the holder later lives or works. They notify that authority of the new address."),
("A personal licence holder is charged with a relevant offence. What must they do at their first court appearance?",
 "Tell the court about the licence", ["Hand the licence to the police", "Ask for the case to be moved", "Stop working until the trial"],
 "The holder must produce the licence to the court or tell the court they hold one, no later than their first appearance. This lets the court consider forfeiture or suspension on conviction."),
("A personal licence holder is convicted of a relevant offence. What may the court order?",
 "Forfeiture or suspension", ["Penalty points on it", "A fine for the council", "Transfer to the employer"],
 "On conviction the court may order the personal licence to be forfeited or suspended for up to six months. Penalty points belong to driving licences, not personal licences."),
("What is the longest period for which a court can suspend a personal licence?",
 "Six months", ["Three months", "One year", "Two years"],
 "A court can suspend a personal licence for up to six months or order it to be forfeited. A licensing authority's own suspension power is also capped at six months."),
("Since 2017, who else can suspend or revoke a personal licence after a relevant conviction?",
 "The licensing authority", ["The police alone", "The premises licence holder", "Trading standards"],
 "The Policing and Crime Act 2017 lets the licensing authority that issued a licence suspend it for up to six months or revoke it after a relevant conviction, even if the court did not."),
("Which of these is a relevant offence for a personal licence?",
 "Drink driving", ["Speeding", "Littering", "Not paying a parking fine"],
 "Relevant offences are listed in Schedule 4 to the Act and include drink driving, theft, fraud, violence, drugs and sexual offences. Ordinary speeding and parking matters are not on the list."),
("Which of these convictions is a relevant offence for a personal licence holder?",
 "Selling alcohol to a child", ["Using a phone while driving", "Fly-tipping garden waste", "Not wearing a seat belt"],
 "Offences under the Licensing Act itself, including selling alcohol to a person under 18, are relevant offences. The motoring and waste offences listed are not."),
("A personal licence holder is convicted of a relevant offence. Whom must they notify?",
 "The issuing licensing authority", ["Only their current employer", "Only the local police", "The local magistrates' court"],
 "The holder must tell the licensing authority that issued the licence about the conviction as soon as reasonably practicable. Failing to do so is an offence."),
("Is a personal licence holder legally required to be present at every alcohol sale?",
 "No", ["Yes", "Only after 11 pm", "Only on busy nights"],
 "The law requires every sale to be made or authorised by a personal licence holder, but the holder need not be on the premises. Statutory guidance says authorisation should be clear, ideally in writing."),
("A police officer asks a personal licence holder on duty to produce their licence. What must they do?",
 "Show it straight away", ["Post it within 28 days", "Show it within 7 days", "Refuse without a warrant"],
 "A holder on the premises to make or authorise sales must produce the licence for examination when a constable or authorised officer asks. Failing to do so without a reasonable excuse is an offence."),
("What should you do if your personal licence is stolen?",
 "Report it and ask for a copy", ["Apply for a new licence", "Retake the qualification", "Use your manager's licence"],
 "Report the theft to the police, then apply to the issuing authority for a copy for a small fee. The licence itself is still valid, so no new application or qualification is needed."),
("Who can authorise the sale of alcohol under a premises licence?",
 "A personal licence holder", ["Any staff member over 18", "The duty manager on shift", "The licensed door supervisor"],
 "A mandatory condition says every supply of alcohol under a premises licence must be made or authorised by a personal licence holder. A duty manager without one cannot authorise sales."),
("Where can a personal licence holder authorise sales of alcohol?",
 "At any licensed premises", ["Only at one named venue", "Only in their home council", "Only where they are DPS"],
 "A personal licence is portable: it lets the holder authorise sales at any premises with a premises licence for alcohol, anywhere in England and Wales."),
("Which convictions must an applicant declare on a personal licence application?",
 "Unspent relevant offences", ["Every conviction ever", "Only prison sentences", "Only offences this year"],
 "Applicants must declare relevant and foreign offences that are not spent under the Rehabilitation of Offenders Act 1974. Spent convictions do not need to be disclosed."),
("Can a holder whose personal licence is suspended authorise alcohol sales?",
 "No, not while suspended", ["Yes, under supervision", "Yes, in the daytime only", "Yes, if the DPS agrees"],
 "A suspended licence has no effect for the suspension period, so the holder cannot authorise sales or act as DPS until it ends."),
("Who awards the licensing qualification needed for a personal licence?",
 "An accredited awarding body", ["The council licensing committee", "The local police licensing unit", "The Home Office licensing team"],
 "The qualification is issued by an awarding body accredited by the Secretary of State. The council then grants the licence itself."),
("How many passport-style photographs normally accompany a personal licence application?",
 "Two", ["One", "Three", "Four"],
 "The regulations ask for two photographs, one of them endorsed as a true likeness by a professional person such as a solicitor or teacher."),
("What happens to a personal licence if the holder stops working in the licensed trade?",
 "It stays valid", ["It lapses after a year", "It is suspended", "It must be surrendered"],
 "A personal licence is not tied to a job or premises. It stays valid until the holder surrenders it or it is revoked or forfeited."),
("How should a personal licence holder authorise staff to sell alcohol?",
 "In writing, as good practice", ["Verbally, at each shift", "By text message each time", "No authorisation is needed"],
 "The law does not say how authorisation must be given, but statutory guidance recommends written authorisation so staff and enforcement officers can see who may sell."),
]
